#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125763 :tutorial/content "A set of vectors \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}\n$$\n\nis called a **basis** of an abstract vector space $V$ if the following two conditions hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent, and\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V.$\n\nFor example, let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degree at most $2,$ and its subset \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{ {\\color{blue}{1}}, {\\color{blue}{t}}, {\\color{blue}{t^2}} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIs $\\mathcal{B}$ a basis for $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]?$ Let's check our two conditions.\n\n- First, we check for linear independence. Notice that the equation \n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} + x_2 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t} + x_3 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0\n$$\n\n \nis true for all $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\nx_1=x_2=x_3=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$\n\n- Next, we check that $\\mathcal B$ spans $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$ Notice that we can write any polynomial $at^2+bt+c\\in\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ as a linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nat^2+bt+c=c\\cdot{\\color{blue}1} + b\\cdot{\\color{blue}t} + a\\cdot{\\color{blue}t^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathcal B$ spans $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ {1}, {t}, {t^2} \\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d94c7bd-3777-5331-b633-0e2463092e26" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3210 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125764 :tutorial/content "A set of vectors $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ is a basis of a vector space $V$ if $\\mathcal{B}$ is a **maximally linearly independent set in $V$**. In other words, $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent and for any vector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\in V,$ the set \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} \\cup \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\} = \\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\}\n$$\n\nis linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "dc0c1b8c-4abf-5ce9-aac8-763330c5a699" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3211 :tutorial/title "An Alternative Definition"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125765 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ denote the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb{R}$ that have degree at most $n.$ Also, let $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ be the vector space of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$ Then, we have the following important standard bases.\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2 \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is\n$$\n\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fd76fc85-4c43-564f-96ac-8492b69f8ecf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3212 :tutorial/title "Some Important Standard Bases"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133552} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0341b26-9c91-5b8a-a917-20129155e336" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048897}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186664802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186664833}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Row Operation Equivalent To Left Multiplication by an Elementary Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133553} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09d8ec89-4d75-51ac-a870-1d2f8a1800ba" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186583637}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Left Multiplication Equivalent To a Given Row Operation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133554} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b69189-1ae1-5625-adfb-e5460845c776" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186583599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186583649}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Left Multiplication Equivalent To a Sequence of Row Operations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133911 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133466} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28142e26-f6c7-54c9-9c16-035c75f7659f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049159}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572700}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With A Zero Row/Column or Two Identical Rows/Columns"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133912 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133467} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aef330a-81b3-56c4-9559-67df673e8687" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484193}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Two Proportional Rows/Columns"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133468} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572686}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135024 :tutorial/content "Applying a row operation to any $3\\times 3$ matrix is the same as *pre-multiplying* (multiplying on the left) the matrix by the corresponding elementary matrix.\n\nFor instance, if we wish to swap the first row and the second row of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ we pre-multiply $A$ by the corresponding elementary matrix \n$$\nE_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]} \n\t\\qquad\n\t(R_1\\leftrightarrow R_2)\n$$\nto get $E_1 A.$\n\nTo perform a sequence of row operations to a $3\\times 3$ matrix, we pre-multiply the matrix by the elementary matrix corresponding to the first operation, and then pre-multiply the result by the elementary matrix corresponding to the second operation.\n\nFor instance, given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A,$ suppose that we would like to multiply the second row by $2$ and then add $3$ times the first row to the second row. To do this, we first pre-multiply $A$ by \n$$\nE_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]} \n\t\\qquad\n\t(R_2 := 2R_2).\n$$\nto get $E_2 A.$ Then we pre-multiply the result by \n$$\nE_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]} \n\t\\qquad\n\t(R_2 := R_2 + 3R_1)\n$$ \nto get $E_3 E_2 A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c110a2ca-8856-5b26-9ded-2e9f35023747" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3171 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135151 :tutorial/content "If an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ has an entire row or column of zeros, then its determinant is equal to zero.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A$ given below:\n\n$$\nA=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe second row consists of all zeros, and the asterisks ($\\ast$) may represent any number.\n\nLet's compute the determinant using the Laplace expansion across the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2+1}(0)\\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)^{2+2}(0)\\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)^{2+3}(0)\\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+0+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that it doesn't matter what numbers are represented by the asterisks ($\\ast$). Each cofactor is multiplied by zero, so the final result must be zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "cee0a552-60f2-5785-a506-63bd053364c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6888 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135152 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose a matrix has no zero rows but it has two identical rows:\n\n$$\nA  = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}1} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe can subtract the $1$st row from the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this operation does not change the determinant, we get $\\det(A) = \\det(B).$ Furthermore, as $B$ contains a zero row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = \\det(B) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can use this method with columns too. Therefore, we have the following property:\n\n*If a square matrix has at least two identical rows/columns, its determinant equals zero.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "6813d49e-3908-5735-8196-8e1aad5a14e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6889 :tutorial/title "Determinants with Identical Rows or Identical Columns"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135153 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose a matrix has no zero rows and no identical rows but it has two proportional rows:\n\n$$\nA  = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}1} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{blue}- 3} & {\\color{blue}- 6} & {\\color{blue}- 3}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nWe can add three times the $1$st row to the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this operation does not change the determinant, we get $\\det(A) = \\det(B).$ Furthermore, as $B$ contains a zero row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = \\det(B) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can use this method with columns too. Therefore, we have the following property:\n\n*If a square matrix has at least two proportional rows/columns, its determinant equals zero.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe83e8c9-948a-596f-a2d9-c2ae2fe4f335" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6890 :tutorial/title "Determinants with Proportional Rows/Columns"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135154 :tutorial/content "In conclusion, if an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ satisfies any one of the following conditions, then $\\det(A)=0.$\n\nI. $A$ has a zero row/column\n\nII. $A$ has two identical rows/columns\n\nIII. $A$ has two proportional rows/columns\n\nIV. $A$ has a row/column that is a linear combination of other rows/columns\n\nNotice that condition IV encompasses each of the conditions I, II, and III. If we have a zero row/column, two identical rows/columns, or proportional rows/columns, then the rows/columns form a linearly dependent set of vectors. \nIn turn, this means that one of the rows/columns is a linear combination of other rows/columns.\n\n**Watch out!** While all of the above conditions are *sufficient* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0,$ only condition IV is *necessary* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0.$ \nIn other words, if we have a matrix $A$ with $\\det(A)=0,$ then conditions I, II, and III may or may not be satisfied, while condition IV *must* be satisfied. \n\nSo, we have the following theorem:\n\n*$\\det(A)=0$ if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nFinally, note also that in the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix *only*, statements III and IV mean the same thing." :tutorial/id #uuid "9d8b0323-170c-5a60-b372-1a258c4848cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6891 :tutorial/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135155 :tutorial/content "Geometrically, the absolute value of an $n \\times n$ determinant can be interpreted as the volume of the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of the determinant. \n\nFor example:\n\n- Suppose the columns of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ are ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{b}},$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{c}}.$\n$$\nA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{black}|} & {\\color{black}|} & {\\color{black}|} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}} & {\\color{black}\\mathbf{b}} & {\\color{black}\\mathbf{c}} \\\\ {\\color{black}|} & {\\color{black}|} & {\\color{black}|}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nThe $3$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of $A$ is depicted below:\n\n![](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nThe volume $V$ of this parallelepiped equals the absolute value of the determinant of $A{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\left|\\det(A)\\right|\n$$\n\n- Now suppose the columns of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ are ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{p}}$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}}.$\n$$\nB = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{black}|} & {\\color{black}|} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\mathbf{p}} & {\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}} \\\\ {\\color{black}|} & {\\color{black}|}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nThe $2$-dimensional parallelepiped (i.e., the parallelogram) spanned by vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/4a/4af1971404b188df75bb3d62d47ac95340c5e1791066e5358370d743230baa45.png)\n\nThe two-dimensional volume $V$ (i.e., the area) of this parallelepiped equals the absolute value of the determinant of $B{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\left|\\det(B)\\right|\n$$\n\n- In general, the absolute value of the determinant of an $n\\times n$ matrix $M$ gives the $n$-dimensional volume of the parallelepiped spanned by the columns of $M.$\n\nIn this lesson, we studied the following property:\n\n*$\\det(A) = 0$ if and only if one of the columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other columns of $A$.*\n\nWhat does this property mean in geometric terms?\n\nThe component $\\det(A) = 0$ implies that the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped formed by the columns of $A$ has zero volume. Conversely, when one of the columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other columns, it indicates that the corresponding parallelepiped possesses at most $n-1$ distinct dimensions. \n\nConsider the following examples:\n\n- In the case of a $3$-dimensional parallelepiped, if the vector $\\mathbf{c}$ is a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$, then $\\mathbf{c}$ lies within the plane containing $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. In other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$, $\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\mathbf{c}$ are coplanar. Consequently, we obtain a \"flat\" ($2$-dimensional) figure with a volume of $0$.\n\n- For the parallelogram example, if the vector $\\mathbf{q}$ is a linear combination of the vector $\\mathbf{p}$ (i.e., $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are proportional), then $\\mathbf{q}$ resides on the line containing vector $\\mathbf{p}$. In other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are collinear. This results in a $1$-dimensional object with a $2$-dimensional volume (i.e., area) of $0$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f7191c6-60ca-5c44-befc-8dbad7779b08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6893 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of a Zero Determinant"}]}
